{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"7d4fdbb8-b00a-496d-87ea-5efe80647ec0","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Solve two x plus three less than nine. The number line will show which values of x work.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"original","region":"A1","latex":"2x+3<9"},{"op":"diagram","id":"numberline","region":"right","primitives":[{"kind":"line","points":[[0.1,0.6],[0.92,0.6]],"stroke":"solid","arrow":true,"label":"x"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.35,0.58],[0.35,0.62]],"stroke":"solid"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.65,0.58],[0.65,0.62]],"stroke":"solid"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.35,0.68],"content":"0"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.65,0.68],"content":"3"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"7d4fdbb8-b00a-496d-87ea-5efe80647ec0","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Subtract three from both sides. Then divide by positive two, so the inequality sign stays the same.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"subtract","anchor":{"el":"original","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"2x<6"},{"op":"equation","id":"solution","anchor":{"el":"subtract","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"x<3"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"7d4fdbb8-b00a-496d-87ea-5efe80647ec0","step":{"id":"s3","script":"An open circle excludes three. The arrow points left because every number less than three satisfies the inequality.","ops":[{"op":"angle_arc","id":"openend","canvas_id":"numberline","center":[0.65,0.48],"radius":0.025,"start_deg":0,"end_deg":360,"stroke":"solid"},{"op":"arrow","id":"solutions","canvas_id":"numberline","from":[0.625,0.48],"to":[0.12,0.48],"stroke":"solid","label":"x < 3"},{"op":"line","id":"boundaryguide","canvas_id":"numberline","from":[0.65,0.515],"to":[0.65,0.575],"stroke":"dashed"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"Is three included in the solution? Why?","expected_gist":"No. The inequality is strict: three makes both sides equal, not less than."}}}
